4. But what if we want to evaluate all relevant records?
Excellent question to ask next! MRR alone won't tell you whether all three relevant records were retrieved.

For your medical retrieval system, these metrics serve different purposes:
Metric	What it measures
MRR@k	How early the first relevant record appears
Precision@k	What proportion of the top-k records are relevant
Recall@k	What proportion of all known relevant records were retrieved
MAP@k	How well relevant records are ranked throughout the top-k results

For your example, all three relevant records appear in the top five, but the first relevant result is at rank 2. So MRR@5 is \(0.5\), while Recall@5 is \(3/3=1.0\).

Remember: MRR rewards finding the first relevant record early. If you want to evaluate the ranking of multiple relevant records, metrics such as MAP@k are more appropriate.
